    title: "The real Thane decision is rarely 'which industry' - it is commute-to-Mumbai versus stay-and-work-local",
    body: "Thane railway station is one of the busiest stations on the Mumbai suburban network, and a large share of Thane's working population makes that daily run into BKC, Lower Parel, Powai, or South Mumbai for pay that a comparable local role in Wagle Estate, Kolshet Road, or Ghodbunder Road usually cannot yet match. That is a genuinely different pressure from Mumbai's own commute problem, where the choice is mostly which part of Mumbai to commute within. In Thane, the choice is sharper: trade two to three hours a day and a harder daily grind for a stronger Mumbai salary, or accept a lower-paying but local Thane role and get that time and energy back.",
  },
  {
    title: 'Neither side of that trade-off is automatically the right answer',
    body: "A Mumbai-based role can be worth the commute when the pay gap is large, the skill exposure is genuinely stronger, or the role sits on a career track that Thane's own corridor cannot yet offer. A local Thane role can be worth the lower pay when the time and energy saved go into building a stronger skill portfolio, a side income, or family stability that a punishing commute would otherwise eat into. The honest answer depends on the specific role, the specific pay gap, and what the saved time would actually be used for - not a blanket rule in either direction.",
  },
  {
    title: 'Thane\'s own corridor is growing, which makes this decision more genuinely open than it was a few years ago',
    body: "As Wagle Estate, Kolshet Road, and the Ghodbunder Road belt keep adding office and IT-park capacity, the pay and role quality available without a Mumbai commute keeps improving - the honest comparison a few years ago and the honest comparison today are not the same, and guidance that treats this as a static, permanently obvious answer is out of date. The practical work is a fair year-by-year read of Thane's own opportunity against the specific Mumbai role in question, not a fixed assumption that Mumbai always wins.",
  },
];

const areaItems = [
